    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-24-250w",
    "note": "Published row: \"Total PoE available = 250W\". Per-port ceiling \"PoE+: 34.2W\" (802.3at Type 2 at 50-57V); 24V passive PoE is 17W per port on the same jacks and is not additive with the 802.3at budget. Max power consumption 30W excludes PoE output. The 250 W is a PSE allocation printed beside the 250 W internal PSU, not a PSU rating read as a ceiling."
  },
  "notes": "Census from the vendor tech-spec table (https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-24-250w, HTTP 200, 583,341 B, server-rendered table, read 2026-11-20): Networking interface \"(24) GbE RJ45 ports; (2) 1G SFP ports\"; PoE interface \"(24) PoE/PoE+ 2-pair (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-); (24) 24V DC passive PoE 2-pair\"; Management interface \"(1) RJ45 serial port, Ethernet In/Out-Band\". As written before this pass the row had 24 plain 1G ports with no connector, no poeOut and no budget, so an AP on Port 5 was refused power by a switch whose own page prints 250 W - the same defect class as #2244/#2249. The serial console was absent (26 jacks modelled for 27 physical), and the SFP cages named no connector. Speed ladder: the page prints \"GbE RJ45\" and no 10M rung, so 1G/100M only."
